<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" style="text-align: justify;">
                <h2><b>Real-Time vs. Batch Integration in Dynamics 365: Which is Right for You?</b></h2>
                <p>
                    Choosing between real-time and scheduled or batch processing depends on business urgency, data volume, system capacity, and the consequences of delayed updates. iFOX evaluates these factors during integration consulting so each process uses an appropriate and supportable pattern.
                </p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Real-Time Integration: Suitable for processes where an immediate response matters, such as updating customer activity or triggering a related workflow after an important event.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Scheduled or Batch Integration: Suitable for non-urgent exchanges, larger data sets, or periodic reporting where processing at defined intervals can simplify operations and manage system load.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Hybrid Processing: Use different patterns for different processes when some information needs prompt exchange while other data can be handled on a schedule.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Capacity and Reliability: Consider transaction volume, rate limits, dependencies, retry behavior, and monitoring requirements before selecting a processing pattern.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Use-Case Analysis: Match the integration pattern to the business process, technical feasibility, data ownership, and support responsibilities.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />
                <h2><b>Enhancing Business Intelligence with Dynamics 365 Integration?</b></h2>
                <p>
                    Integrating Dynamics 365 with business intelligence tools can improve operational visibility when data is mapped, governed, and refreshed in a way that fits reporting needs. Connected CRM and ERP information can help teams review activity, identify trends, and make decisions using a clearer view of business operations.
                </p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Business Intelligence Integration: Connect suitable Dynamics 365, CRM, ERP, and other business data to support reports and dashboards that reflect agreed operational needs.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Data Consolidation: Bring together relevant data from CRM, ERP, and other systems while documenting mappings, ownership, and refresh expectations.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Suitable Analytics Workflows: Assess reporting and analytics requirements without assuming that every integration requires predictive models or AI capabilities.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Reporting Refresh: Define appropriate refresh timing and validation checks so dashboards and reports provide useful, consistent information for decision-making.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Data Quality and Consistency: Ensure that data used for business intelligence is consistent, clean, and updated regularly, enhancing the accuracy and reliability of insights.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-4 Imgborder">
                <div><img src="~/assets/img/dynamics365/Developer2.webp" class="img-fluid text-start ms-0 mb-5 mt-3 me-3 p-3" alt="Developer working on a computer" /></div>
